  "style": {
    "id": "paper-marbling",
    "name": "Paper Marbling",
    "description": "Paper marbling in a shallow white enamel tray on a bright pale-wood studio table, seen from above with a slight tilt. A glass pipette lets drops of ink fall onto thickened water; each drop spreads into a flat disc and pushes every earlier colour outward into concentric rings; a stylus is drawn back and forth and a comb is pulled across, and the rings stretch into feathers and chevrons; a sheet of paper is rolled onto the bath, peeled away carrying the pattern (the bath is left almost clean), lifted and turned to the camera as a finished print. The pattern is not simulated: it is a composition of closed-form area-preserving marbling deformations (ink-drop displacement, tine-line and comb displacement, from the mathematical marbling literature) applied in plan order; a fragment shader inverse-maps each pixel through the operations so far, the current one partially applied by its progress, so any frame is a pure function of t. Flat saturated inks (indigo, vermilion, ochre, teal, cream) with a slightly denser rim per drop; soft daylight; water plips, soft drags and paper sounds come from the same plan.",
